\begin{title}
Microstructure and Magnetic Properties of FeNiCuMnTiSnx High Entropy Alloys**
\end{title}

By Liang Liu, Jian Bo Zhu, Jian Chen Li and Qing Jiang

FeNiCuMnTiSnx multicomponent alloys (x ¼ 0, 0.05, 0.1, 0.5, and 1 where x denotes the adding amount in atomic percentage when other elements are taken as 1) are prepared by an arc furnace. The results show that the crystal structure of FeNiCuMnTiSnx alloys transforms from mixed intermetallic compounds to a single crystal structure (zinc blende structure) with the increasing of Sn content. The magnetization of the FeNiCuMnTiSnx alloys gradually increases from 0.34 to 15.8 emu g 1 with the increasing x, the magnetic transformation undergoes from the paramagnetism (x ¼ 0, 0.05, 0.1) to superparamagnetism (x ¼ 0.5) ﬁnally to soft magnetism (x ¼ 1) at room temperature. We use computer simulation and ﬁnd that the magnetism of alloys appears when Fe atoms replace Ni atoms.

Conventional strategy for developing metallic alloys is to select one or two elements as the main components based on a primary property requirement, such as iron-, copper-, and aluminum-based alloys. In the recent years, in order to break through this cognitive concept of alloy design, a new alloy system was developed,[1–3] this new concept of alloy system is called ‘‘high-entropy alloy.’’ High-entropy alloys have multi- elements as their major constituents. Because there is no matrix element in high-entropy alloy system, each constituent element in this system could be regarded as a solute atom or solvent atom. To increase the entropy, each major element of the alloy systems is in equimolar or near-equimolar ratio. High-entropy alloys have attracted great attentions because they exhibit unique properties compared to its counterpart crystalline materials.[4–7] Just based on the simple structure and unique properties, there has been increasing interests for exploring the equiatomic multicomponent alloys.

In recent years, the magnetic properties of high-entropy alloys are investigated.[8–13] In this composition, a novel FeNiCuMnTiSnx alloys were prepared. Their microstructure and magnetic properties were examined to attempt investiga- tion of effect of Sn element on the microstructure and properties of the alloys.

[*] Dr. L. Liu, J. B. Zhu, Prof. J. C. Li, Prof. Q. Jiang Key Laboratory of Automobile Materials, Ministry of Educa- tion, School of Materials Science and Engineering, Jilin University, Changchun 130025, China E-mail: ljc@jlu.edu.cn

[**] The authors gratefully acknowledge the ﬁnancial supports from NNSFC (Grant No. 50571040) and National Foundation of Doctoral Station (Grant No. 20100061110019).

\begin{title}
1. Experimental
\end{title}

FeNiCuMnTiSnx multicomponent alloys are prepared by a vacuum furnace under argon atmosphere. The purity of all the raw elemental metal is above 99.9%. The rod ingot of the alloy with a diameter of 6 mm is obtained in a cold copper hearth. The corresponding cooling rate is about 103 K s 1. Table 1 shows the composition of the alloys. The phase structure analysis of the samples was made by Rigaku D/max 2500 X-ray diffractometer at 50 KV and 250 mA. Scanning angles ranged from 208 to 1208 with a scanning rate of 58 min 1. The microstructure of the alloys was observed by scanning electron microscope (SEM). Elected area composition analysis of the samples was carried out by an energy dispersive spectrometry (EDS). The hysteresis loops were measured on a VSM-7300 vibrating sample magnetometer (VSM) (Lakeshore, USA) at room temperature. The data of atomic magnetic moment (M) of alloy were calculated by the CASTEP program package in Accelrys Materials Studio software.

\begin{title}
2. Results and Discussion
\end{title}

Figure 1 is the XRD patterns of the FeNiCuMnTiSnx alloys. When no Sn added, FeNiCuMnTi alloy are intermetallic compounds, which are Fe2Ti, NiTi, FeTi, and Fe3Mn7, however, FeNiCuMnTiSn alloy is TiNi2Sn or Cu3Sn phases, which are zinc blende structure as shown the top right corner in Figure 1. Figure 2 presents the microstructure of the alloys with different Sn content. There are two type of principal grain morphology. When Sn content is low (x ¼ 0, 0.05, 0.1), the grain morphology is dendritic crystal structure with an average primary arm width of about 2.5 mm and the grain size is small. When Sn content increases (x ¼ 0.5, 1), the grain

