Table 2 is chemical compositions of FeNiCuMnTi and FeNiCuMnTiSn alloys by EDS. It is obvious that for FeNiCuMnTi alloy Fe is rich in dendrite regions and Cu is rich in interdendrite regions, Ti, Ni, and Mn are distributed evenly both in dendrite and interdendrite regions. Combined with XRD results, the FeNiCuMnTi alloy is mixture of intermetallic compounds of Fe, Ti, Ni, and Mn. But for FeNiCuMnTiSn alloy, Ti, Ni, and Sn are rich in dendrite regions, Cu and Mn are rich in interdendrite regions. It is also noticeable that the atomic percentage rate of Fe, Ti, Ni, and Sn in dendrite region of FeNiCuMnTiSn alloy is approximate 1:1:1:1, suppositionally the phase in dendrite region of FeNiCuMnTiSn alloy would be Ti(NiFe)Sn. It is known that Fe atom is very similar to Ni atom and easy to form substitution solid solution, so the phase in dendrite region would be TiNi2Sn whose crystal structure is completely consistent with the X-ray analysis of Figure 1. While Cu is rich in indendrite region, their atomic percentage rate is approximate 3:1, so that the phase in indendrite region of FeNiCuMnTiSn alloy can be written as Cu3Sn which has the same structure of TiNi2Sn. In summary, FeNiCuMnTiSn alloy is mixed by two phases which are TiNi2Sn phase in dendrite region and Cu3Sn phase in indendrite region, both of which have the same crystal structure.

