In order to comprehend the magnetic transformation of the alloys, we will discuss the relation of the crystal structure and the magnetic transformation of the alloys by the composition analysis and computer simulation. The atomic magnetic moment (M) of alloy is calculated by using the CASTEP program package in Accelrys Materials Studio software. The crystal structure of TiNi2Sn and Cu3Sn are quoted from refs.[15,16 Figure 4 is the crystal structure used in computer simulation. Table 4 shows the simulant data of M of different structures. The M of Cu3Sn and TiNi2Sn are 0.4468 10 4 and 0.2255 10 4 Hbar respectively, which implies that Cu3Sn and TiNi2Sn are nonmagnetic. In order to ﬁnd how magnetism arises, we use Fe atom replace Ni atom in unit cell according to the chemical compositions analysis in Table 1. When one and three Ni atoms are replaced by Fe atoms, the M are 0.4718 10 2 and 0.2260 10 2 Hbar respectively, almost nonmagnetic. While two or four Ni atoms are replaced, the M are 4.2136 Hbar and 8.8981 Hbar, respectively, Ti4(Ni6Fe2) Sn4 and Ti4(Ni4Fe4)Sn4 are magnetic. Based on the above analysis, the magnetism of the FeNiCuMnTiSnx alloys rests

